# agentmux tmux overlays — OPTIONAL, PER-ROLE.
#
# agentmux runs each surface on its OWN tmux server with its OWN `-f` config, none of
# which read your ~/.tmux.conf. Each server sources a matching overlay LAST (via
# `source-file -q`), so your settings override agentmux's defaults. There are THREE,
# one per role — they are NOT shared:
#
#   ~/.agentmux/user.agent.tmux.conf   the agent pane (where you spend ~all your time)
#   ~/.agentmux/user.frame.tmux.conf   the `amux --frame` outer wrapper
#   ~/.agentmux/user.term.tmux.conf    the `--frame` left scratch terminal
#
# WHY PER-ROLE (not one shared file): the three servers use DIFFERENT prefixes and
# bindings — the frame's prefix is C-f and it unbinds the window-management keys to
# hold its fixed two-pane layout. A single shared overlay would apply your `bind-key`
# to all three, so a binding meant for the agent would land on the frame and break it.
# Per-role files keep every binding scoped to the surface you meant.
#
# Copy this file to whichever role(s) you want to customize, e.g.:
#   cp ~/.agentmux/config/user.tmux.conf.example ~/.agentmux/user.agent.tmux.conf
# Most people only need the AGENT overlay. An absent overlay is a silent no-op — you
# don't need any of these files unless you want to customize.
#
# WHY THE ISOLATION EXISTS: a cold per-project agent server that fell through to
# ~/.tmux.conf would run TPM's synchronous plugin load and stall every launch for
# seconds. agentmux re-sets the sensible defaults itself (escape-time, focus-events,
# history-limit, mouse, clipboard). These overlays are the escape hatch past that
# fixed list.
#
# FOOTGUN — don't just re-source your whole config: `source-file ~/.tmux.conf` here
# reintroduces the cold-start stall if that file loads TPM/plugins. Copy the specific
# bindings/options you want instead.
#
# RELOAD: `amux --reload` re-sources the agent config — and therefore
# user.agent.tmux.conf — into every live agent server. Prefer idempotent settings
# (`set -g`, `bind`) over anything that accumulates on repeat sourcing. The frame/term
# overlays are picked up when you relaunch the frame.
#
# SHARE SETTINGS ACROSS ROLES: if you want the same settings everywhere, keep them in
# one file of your own and source it from each role overlay, e.g. add this line to each
# of the three:  source-file -q ~/.agentmux/user.common.tmux.conf
# Put only role-AGNOSTIC settings there (options like mode-keys/history-limit, styles) —
# NOT `bind-key`s. A shared file sourced into the frame re-creates the very cross-role
# binding leak the per-role split fixed: a binding meant for the agent lands on the
# frame's C-f layout and breaks it. Bindings belong in the specific role file.
#
# ── Examples (uncomment / edit; most belong in user.agent.tmux.conf) ──────────────

# vi keys in copy mode
# setw -g mode-keys vi

# a bigger scrollback than agentmux's default
# set -g history-limit 100000

# your own status-bar style (agentmux styles the bar per session; this overrides)
# set -g status-style 'bg=colour234,fg=colour252'

# a custom key binding — scoped to whichever role file you put it in
# bind-key C-r display "hello from my overlay"

# FRAME-ONLY: a custom clipboard pipe (must live in user.frame.tmux.conf to reach
# --frame copies — the frame socket grabs the drag-select first; see frame.conf)
# bind -T copy-mode-vi MouseDragEnd1Pane send-keys -X copy-pipe-and-cancel "pbcopy"
